From d39a228474ea15854f704ebd5cb27648370efd07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kshot3000 <kshot9000@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 07:20:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] refactor(server): return getdata messages from fetch helpers
 instead of queuing them (#297)

The pushInventory helpers each reimplemented 'signal completion exactly
once, after my last message' by hand: they built wire messages, queued
them, picked which message carried the done channel, and signaled the
error paths themselves. Nothing enforced the rule, and one case was
already wrong by construction: pushMerkleBlockMsg attached the channel
to the last matched tx but queued it only inside the range guard, so an
out-of-range last index would have stranded the signal and parked
OnGetData until the peer disconnected.

The fetch helpers (fetchInventory/fetchTx/fetchBlock/fetchMerkleBlock)
now only build []outbound{msg, enc}; OnGetData owns queueing and
attaches the done channel to the last returned message, so a dropped
message means one fewer message, never a stranded receive. The three
return shapes are preserved exactly: (nil, err) -> notfound, no signal;
(nil, nil) -> deliberate no-reply for a peer with no filter loaded, no
notfound; (msgs, nil) -> queue all, exactly one signal. Encodings are
unchanged per message (block and matched txs use the caller's encoding,
the continue inv and merkleblock use base encoding), as is the
continueHash side effect in fetchBlock.

Tests: TestFetchInventoryShapes pins the (nil, err) and (nil, nil)
shapes through fetchInventory; TestMerkleOutbound covers merkle
assembly including a last matched index out of range. Full node package
suite passes untouched.
